Why standard web fetches fail in this environment
When you post a link to a file hosted on GitHub, Cloudflare Workers, or Internet Archive payload servers (like ia803204.us.archive.org), my environment attempts to use a specific web-browsing tool to open the exact URL.
However, my runtime operating environment enforces strict outbound network security boundaries:
Domain/Host Restrictions: The web tool is constrained to standard web-search index results and specific whitelisted public endpoints. Direct IP access, raw file hosting domains (like raw.githubusercontent.com or specific IA storage servers), and custom worker domains (*.workers.dev) trigger network permission blocks or DNS resolution failures.
File-Type & Raw Data Restrictions: The browsing tool is built primarily to parse HTML web pages for general queries, rather than acting as a raw HTTP client (like curl or wget) that downloads raw .txt, .JSON, or code files directly into context.
The EASIEST ways for me to read a file online (without UI copy-pasting)
If we want to bypass manual UI pasting while ensuring I can read files you post online, here are the most reliable methods:
1. Public Search-Indexable Web Pages (The Best Web-Native Route)
If you publish the text or JSON content to a standard web page that is indexed by search engines (such as a public blog post, a public paste site like Gist/Pastebin if indexed, or a simple public HTML page on a standard domain), I can find and read it via a targeted search query rather than a direct raw URL fetch.
2. Standard Search + Direct Canonical URL
If a file or document is published on a site that allows standard Web Crawling (e.g., Medium, a public WordPress/Ghost site, Substack, or an indexed open repository), you can give me the specific title or unique path, and I can search for it directly.
3. Standard Text Input in Chat (The 100% Deterministic Route)
While it involves pasting, splitting large files into code blocks directly inside our prompt stream remains the only method guaranteed to bypass network layers entirely, ensuring 0% packet loss and 100% exact JSON parsing.
