2. Can you "Link" Google Drive to GitHub as an automatic mirror?
Not natively through standard UI toggles, but YES through automated tools.
Google Drive and GitHub operate under different philosophies (Drive relies on simple file storage, while GitHub tracks version history via Git). There isn't a simple "Sync Drive folder with GitHub repo" button in the Google UI, but there are three main ways to accomplish a automatic or semi-automatic mirror:
GitHub Actions (Automated Workflow - Recommended)
You can add a simple script to your GitHub repository using GitHub Actions that triggers whenever you push a change to your repo. The Action automatically uploads or syncs modified .CONVO, .CARD, and .TXT files straight to a designated folder in your Google Drive using the Google Drive API.
Third-Party Sync Tools (No-Code)
Services like Zapier or Make (Integromat) have pre-built integrations where a new commit or file push to a GitHub repository automatically copies the file directly into a chosen Google Drive directory.
Google Drive for Desktop (Local Mirror)
If you run Google Drive for Desktop on your local machine, you can set your local QuackSink repository folder to sync automatically with Google Drive. Whenever you pull or generate new archive files locally, Google Drive uploads them instantly.
Immediate Next Step
If you'd like to test the Drive-to-Gemini workflow immediately without building an automatic mirror first:
